> A text reader that takes the largest value the trace wrote recovers more of the gap than the model's own answer (Table~\ref{tab:main}, Figure~\ref{fig:main}c), but those figures are in-sample, and on the synthetic traces it hurts four models, so it is no replacement for the answer. It works as an audit: sending decisions where reader and answer disagree to the calculator recovers 73--93\% of the gap on products not used to set its rule and 82--100\% on synthetic traces, and it beats random, low-confidence and unfinished-list triggers at the same call rate. Why not always call the calculator? A bidder who builds its own agent should. The audit is for the other side of the market: a platform, marketplace or advertiser that hosts or oversees a third-party agent sees its reasoning and its bid but cannot change its prompt or tools. For that overseer a disagreement between the answer and the agent's own numbers is a cheap flag, and on our data it catches most of the lost value.
